 # Every environment variable the API reads. Parsed and validated by api/src/config.ts
# at startup, the process exits with a readable message if a value is missing or invalid.
# Copy to .env for local development. .env is git ignored.
#
# This file is the whole configuration surface and it is deliberately committed, because a
# variable nobody documented is a variable somebody guesses. It holds names and safe defaults
# and never a value: every secret is supplied at runtime, either as an environment variable or
# as a file the deployment mounts and points env_file at, see docker-compose.yml, which is the
# better of the two because anything that can run `docker inspect` can read an environment and
# a file can be 600.
#
# Nothing here is a secret today except AI_API_KEY at the bottom, which is optional. Adding one
# means adding it here by name, with the value left empty. The startup log masks any value whose
# key contains KEY, SECRET, TOKEN, PASSWORD or CREDENTIAL, so the name is what makes it safe.
# Committing a real one is refused by .githooks/pre-commit and by the `secrets` job in CI; if
# one gets through anyway, rotate it rather than deleting the line, see SECURITY.md.

# Runtime mode. One of: development, test, production.
NODE_ENV=development

# TCP port the HTTP server listens on. Integer, 1 to 65535.
PORT=3000

# Log verbosity. One of: fatal, error, warn, info, debug, trace. Lines are JSON, one per line,
# on stdout. Passwords, tokens, addresses in full and model prompts are redacted whatever the
# level, see api/src/http/logging.ts.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# Serves the Swagger UI at /api/v1/docs. The generated spec at /api/v1/openapi.json is
# always served regardless. Anything Zod reads as a boolean: true/false, 1/0, yes/no, on/off.
API_DOCS_ENABLED=true

# Path to the SQLite file. Created on first start together with its parent directory,
# alongside the -wal and -shm sidecars. data/ is git ignored.
DATABASE_PATH=./data/portionium.db

# Directory holding the built web client. When it is set, the API process serves those files on
# its own origin and answers a path it does not recognise with the app shell, so the client's own
# routes survive a reload. Empty means it serves the API only, which is what development wants:
# there the client runs on the Vite dev server and proxies /api here. The container sets it to
# /app/web/dist.
WEB_ROOT=

# The single origin the web client is served from, scheme and host and port. Two things read
# it. It is the CSRF check: a mutating request carrying a session cookie must name this origin
# in its Origin header, and one naming a foreign origin or none at all is refused. Its scheme
# also decides whether the session cookie is marked Secure, so plain http here means a cookie a
# browser will store during development and https means one that never crosses a clear channel.
#
# Because of that second half, NODE_ENV=production plus plain http against a public host is
# refused at startup rather than served: it is a session cookie in clear on an untrusted
# network. Loopback and private addresses over http are fine and stay fine, a home network is
# what this is for. For a domain, use https, the caddy profile in docker-compose.yml gets the
# certificate on its own.
WEB_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# How long a session survives without being used. Refreshed on activity, so it is an idle
# timeout rather than a lifetime: somebody using the app daily is never signed out. 1 to 365.
SESSION_TTL_DAYS=30

# How long a stored Idempotency-Key answers a retry with the original response. After this the
# row is purged and the key may be reused for a new request. 1 to 168 hours.
IDEMPOTENCY_RETENTION_HOURS=24

# Requests one caller may make per minute, counted per credential and per IP address, in three
# buckets. A read is cheap, a write is a transaction and an fsync, a sign in is an Argon2id
# verification at 19 MiB and is the most expensive thing this process does. Counters live in
# process memory and reset when it restarts, see docs/adr/005-no-redis-no-metrics-stack.md.
# Neither probe, /health or /ready, is ever rate limited.
RATE_LIMIT_READ_PER_MINUTE=120
RATE_LIMIT_WRITE_PER_MINUTE=30
RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_PER_MINUTE=20

# Largest request body accepted, in bytes. Anything larger is refused with 413 before it is
# read into memory. A megabyte is about two orders of magnitude more than the biggest thing
# this API takes today, which is a meal with a long list of items.
MAX_BODY_BYTES=1048576

# Origins allowed to call this API from a browser it was not served by. Empty means no CORS
# headers are sent at all, which is the default and the right answer when the web client and
# the API share an origin. Otherwise a comma separated list of exact origins, scheme and host
# and port. There is no wildcard: every response here is credentialed.
CORS_ORIGINS=

# How far a weight reading may drift from the nearest one on record, as a fraction of it per
# day between them, before POST /weight flags it as a warning rather than saying nothing. Never
# blocks the write. 0 to 1, two percent by default.
WEIGHT_MAX_DRIFT_PER_DAY=0.02

# How long a weight reading takes to lose half its influence on the smoothed trend, in days.
# Lower tracks the scale more closely and reports more of its noise as progress, higher is
# calmer and lags further behind. 1 to 60, ten days by default.
WEIGHT_TREND_HALF_LIFE_DAYS=10

# Directory the scheduled backup writes to. Empty means no backups are taken, which is right for
# development and for tests, where the database is a scratch file. The container sets it to
# /data/backups, on the same volume as the database: that survives the container, the image and a
# bad migration, which is what actually goes wrong, and not the disk. Restoring, and getting a
# copy off the machine, are in docs/runbooks/backup.md. A backup is taken with VACUUM INTO and
# never with a file copy, see api/src/db/backup.ts for why that distinction is not pedantry.
BACKUP_DIR=

# How old the newest archive has to be before another is taken. Checked hourly and at startup,
# against the directory rather than against anything the process remembers, so an instance that
# is redeployed every morning still gets a backup and one in a crash loop does not get a hundred.
# 1 to 168 hours.
BACKUP_INTERVAL_HOURS=24

# How many archives survive, per tier, grandfather-father-son. An archive counts in every tier it
# is the newest of, so these three keep about eleven files: a week of daily detail, a couple of
# older weeks and the last day of the months before this one. BACKUP_KEEP_DAILY has a floor of 1,
# which is what stops any policy reaching the archive that was just taken. The other two may be 0.
BACKUP_KEEP_DAILY=7
BACKUP_KEEP_WEEKLY=4
BACKUP_KEEP_MONTHLY=3

# The credential for the model that classifies a food the catalog does not have. Optional, and
# empty here on purpose. Without it that path is simply off: the seeded catalog answers almost
# everything and a food nobody recognises is one you give a colour to yourself. Startup says
# which of the two it is, in one line, so an instance without a key does not read as a broken
# one. This is a secret. Put it in .env.docker or a mounted file, never in this file.
AI_API_KEY=
